1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:09,145
On 20 May 1498, three battered ships dropped anchor off the coast of Kerala, near Calicut.

2
00:00:09,445 --> 00:00:13,340
They had sailed from Portugal, all the way around Africa.

3
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:17,248
No European had ever reached India by sea.

4
00:00:17,598 --> 00:00:21,580
So why did a small kingdom risk everything to get here?

5
00:00:21,830 --> 00:00:24,989
The answer starts with a tiny black seed.

6
00:00:25,939 --> 00:00:26,919
Pepper.

7
00:00:27,269 --> 00:00:31,072
It grew on vines along India's Malabar coast.

8
00:00:31,172 --> 00:00:36,580
With it came cinnamon from Sri Lanka, and cloves from the far-off Spice Islands.

9
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:42,917
In Europe, spices flavoured the food of the rich, filled medicine chests, and announced wealth.

10
00:00:43,667 --> 00:00:46,892
But Europeans never bought them at the source.

11
00:00:47,092 --> 00:00:52,694
Spices crossed the Arabian Sea in the ships of Arab and Gujarati merchants,

12
00:00:52,694 --> 00:00:57,127
sailed up the Red Sea to Egypt, through Cairo to Alexandria,

13
00:00:57,127 --> 00:01:01,104
and there, Venetian traders carried them on into Europe.

14
00:01:01,354 --> 00:01:04,442
Every hand along the way took its cut.

15
00:01:04,492 --> 00:01:11,135
By the time pepper reached a European table, its price had multiplied many times over.

16
00:01:11,385 --> 00:01:14,915
Egypt and Venice held the keys to that trade.

17
00:01:14,965 --> 00:01:21,254
And after 1453, the rising Ottoman Empire loomed over the eastern routes too.

18
00:01:22,105 --> 00:01:27,097
Portugal sat at the far western edge of Europe, locked out of all of it.

19
00:01:27,247 --> 00:01:29,927
But it faced the open Atlantic.

20
00:01:30,177 --> 00:01:36,916
Encouraged by Prince Henry, Portuguese sailors crept down the coast of Africa, year after year.

21
00:01:37,066 --> 00:01:40,353
Cape Bojador in 1434.

22
00:01:40,403 --> 00:01:43,685
The Congo River in 1482.

23
00:01:43,885 --> 00:01:49,876
Then, in 1488, Bartolomeu Dias rounded the southern tip of Africa.

24
00:01:50,126 --> 00:01:53,187
The door to the Indian Ocean was open.

25
00:01:54,136 --> 00:01:57,990
Nine years later, Vasco da Gama sailed through it.

26
00:01:58,190 --> 00:02:00,262
But his route looked strange.

27
00:02:00,412 --> 00:02:07,477
Instead of hugging the coast, he swung far out into the Atlantic, and saw no land for about three months.

28
00:02:07,777 --> 00:02:11,033
Why sail away from Africa, to get around it?

29
00:02:11,383 --> 00:02:13,795
That is where our story begins.
